Bind each occurrence to the reading that explains it (P-283)

An occurrence's reading_id was covered by nothing. Repointing it at another valid
reading left every fingerprint matching, and the structural checks underneath all
pass for the swap that matters: the verdict is defined, the reason is non-empty,
the wording is complete, and the rights still sum to the entry's own mask -
because a deny reading of the same rights has the same bits. So an allow
WRITE_DAC could be made to read as "A deny entry grants no access" and classify
NotDangerous. Dangerous reported as harmless, which is the one thing an audit tool
may not do.

The whole-judgement seal catches an ordinary post-publication edit. It does not
catch a wrong association written before sealing, and P-280 would have turned one
such association into every occurrence of that entry - 209,252 rows on the
laboratory, 330,856,576 on the production capture.

THE READING'S DIGEST IS FOLDED INTO THE OCCURRENCE'S EXISTING FINGERPRINT rather
than given a column of its own. A separate binding would have cost 32 bytes on
every occurrence, which is 10.6 GB across the production capture, in a store
P-280 exists to shrink. The 32 bytes already there now do the job they should
have done. control_ace and full_control_source_entry name no interpretation and
pass nothing, so their fingerprints are unchanged in meaning.

THE READING IS VERIFIED BEFORE IT IS USED TO VERIFY ANYTHING ELSE. Its own digest
is checked against its payload first, and only then is it handed to
SharedPermissionStorage.Read as the material the occurrence is bound to. A value
that has not been proved cannot be evidence that something else is sound - the
same order P-285 established for resolving a trustee name against a target.

JudgementSchema.Version is 45. A version 44 store's occurrence fingerprints did
not cover the reading, so they do not verify against this build and it is refused
on version rather than reported as tampered proofs. The layout checkpoint again
repeats 43's DDL hash, because deliberately no table or index changed.

MEASURED on ad.lab-20260923-231917, against the figures recorded after P-285. The
check written down before the change was "the binding change should alter no count
here at all. A count that moves is the finding."

  permission_entry                   1,929 -> 1,929
  permission_classification_entry  209,252 -> 209,252
  permission_classification_reading    240 -> 240
  finding                               27 -> 27
  rules ran                             17 -> 17
  judgement on disk            95,526,912 -> 95,535,104
  judging wall clock                23.2 s -> 23.0 s

Nothing moved. The store grew by one 8,192-byte page, which is what folding a
value into an existing fingerprint rather than adding a column costs.

TWO CHECKS. The repoint is refused, and - the negative control - the same object
unrepointed reads back with its two entries carrying different readings. The
second is what stops the first passing against a fixture whose two entries shared
one reading row, where the repoint would be a no-op. It asserts on the assessment
rather than the summary because both entries summarise as "Modify permissions":
they carry the same right and only what that right is judged to mean differs.
Written against the summary first, it failed, which is the whole use of it.
Mutation-tested by removing the binding from both the write and the read side,
which reproduces the repoint exactly.

ONE CONSEQUENCE RECORDED RATHER THAN WORKED AROUND. A Shell test rewrites a stored
reading to prove the reader shows recorded words instead of recomputing them, and
repaired the reading's digest so the reader would reach them. That is no longer
enough, because the occurrences bound to that reading move with it. The repair now
rebinds them through SharedPermissionStorage.FingerprintOf - the writer's own key -
rather than through a second copy of it, which could agree with itself while
disagreeing with the product.

